WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.220 --> 00:00:06.319
This is Senegal — home to 15 million people,
one of the region’s relatively more stable

00:00:06.319 --> 00:00:10.990
democracies, and the Westernmost country in
Africa.

00:00:10.990 --> 00:00:16.219
This is Dakar — its capital, and Senegal’s
Westernmost city.

00:00:16.219 --> 00:00:22.929
And within Dakar, this is the African Renaissance
Monument, situated on the very Western edge

00:00:22.929 --> 00:00:25.769
of the Cape Verde Peninsula.

00:00:25.769 --> 00:00:31.730
On the third of April, 2010, this was the
place to be in Africa.

00:00:31.730 --> 00:00:38.300
It was the day before the 50th anniversary
of the country’s independence and 19 presidents

00:00:38.300 --> 00:00:44.190
plus US congressman Jesse Jackson had flown
in to witness the unveiling of this humongous

00:00:44.190 --> 00:00:45.920
bronze statue.

00:00:45.920 --> 00:00:55.740
At 49-meters, or 160-feet high, it was and
still is the tallest statue on the continent,

00:00:55.740 --> 00:01:00.010
taller even, than the Statue of Liberty or
Christ the Redeemer.

00:01:00.010 --> 00:01:07.150
The Socialist Realist-style man, woman, and
child were intended to represent, quote, “Africa

00:01:07.150 --> 00:01:13.490
emerging from darkness, from five centuries
of slavery and two centuries of colonialism”.

00:01:13.490 --> 00:01:20.970
The hill on which it stands, however, is awkwardly
surrounded by squatter camps and garbage dumps,

00:01:20.970 --> 00:01:27.380
which may help explain why today, over a decade
later, it never quite achieved its stated

00:01:27.380 --> 00:01:32.630
goal of rivaling the Eiffel Tower as a tourist
destination.

00:01:32.630 --> 00:01:36.680
There are many strange things about this statue.

00:01:36.680 --> 00:01:43.430
For one, there’s its liberal use of nudity
to represent a 96% Muslim country,

00:01:43.430 --> 00:01:49.860
There’s it's officially $25 but rumored
$70 million dollar price tag, in a country

00:01:49.860 --> 00:01:53.479
where half the population lives below the
poverty line.

00:01:53.479 --> 00:01:58.930
Indeed, thousands of locals protested the
project, calling it an embarrassing testament

00:01:58.930 --> 00:02:04.390
to the government’s corruption, self-obsession,
and backward priorities.

00:02:04.390 --> 00:02:10.160
Then there was the president’s controversial
decision to personally claim 35% of the profits

00:02:10.160 --> 00:02:13.060
to this public monument.

00:02:13.060 --> 00:02:19.790
But far stranger than all of this, is the
fact that, despite a 50% local unemployment

00:02:19.790 --> 00:02:24.420
rate, this entire piece was outsourced to
a foreign firm.

00:02:24.420 --> 00:02:27.600
And not just any foreign firm.

00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:33.160
This massive tribute to African ingenuity
and pride was built by…

00:02:33.160 --> 00:02:35.180
North Korea.

00:02:35.180 --> 00:02:39.819
The African Renaissance Monument isn’t unique
in this respect.

00:02:39.819 --> 00:02:46.630
All over Africa, and even in Europe and elsewhere
are statues, museums, buildings, and monuments

00:02:46.630 --> 00:02:51.180
built entirely by and in North Korea.

00:02:51.180 --> 00:02:56.790
This is, in fact, its single largest export.

00:02:56.790 --> 00:03:02.430
Sponsored by CuriosityStream and Nebula — where
you can watch the extended version of this

00:03:02.430 --> 00:03:04.650
video.

00:03:04.650 --> 00:03:10.879
When the so-called Democratic People's Republic
of Korea was founded in 1948, its existence

00:03:10.879 --> 00:03:18.130
was recognized only by a handful of Communist
countries like the Soviet Union, China, and

00:03:18.130 --> 00:03:19.130
Romania.

00:03:19.130 --> 00:03:23.640
Decades before it began unification talks
with the South, North Korea was looking for

00:03:23.640 --> 00:03:29.170
international legitimacy — a need for which
it was very much willing to overlook just

00:03:29.170 --> 00:03:31.060
about anything.

00:03:31.060 --> 00:03:37.620
Kim Il-Sung was also committed to the ideological
spread of Socialism, actively trying to promote

00:03:37.620 --> 00:03:42.660
the “Juche” philosophy of self-reliance
long before it would be laughed at as a model

00:03:42.660 --> 00:03:44.820
for the rest of the world.

00:03:44.820 --> 00:03:50.819
It was for this reason that this nascent nation
began making allies in Africa.

00:03:50.819 --> 00:03:56.600
It did so by donating money, weapons, and
soldiers, all of which went a long way at

00:03:56.600 --> 00:03:59.110
a time of desperate need.

00:03:59.110 --> 00:04:05.600
The DPRK military, for instance, trained Somali
forces in their fight against Ethiopia, who

00:04:05.600 --> 00:04:09.840
had previously supported the South during
the Korean War.

00:04:09.840 --> 00:04:15.660
A few years later, when Ethiopia’s government
was overthrown by Marxists, North Korea began

00:04:15.660 --> 00:04:21.870
supporting Ethiopia’s fight against Somalia,
both of which established with it diplomatic

00:04:21.870 --> 00:04:23.690
relations.

00:04:23.690 --> 00:04:30.380
In the 70s, North Korea sent thousands of
troops to Angola during its civil war.

00:04:30.380 --> 00:04:35.770
At the same time, at home, the Kim Il-Sung
regime was busy sowing the seeds of what would

00:04:35.770 --> 00:04:42.500
become a propaganda machine rivaling any of
history’s most intense cults of personality.

00:04:42.500 --> 00:04:50.460
This began, rather inconspicuously, with the
founding in 1959 of the Mansudae Art Studio.

00:04:50.460 --> 00:04:56.690
Backed by the Supreme Leader himself, its
task was simple, though not easy: forge the

00:04:56.690 --> 00:05:03.520
public image of a leader who was then dangerously
at risk of being seen as merely human.

00:05:03.520 --> 00:05:09.920
In practical terms, this meant painting murals,
architecting buildings, and sculpting monuments.

00:05:09.920 --> 00:05:16.660
Thanks to its artistic monopoly, virtually
all of Pyongyang’s landmarks are its work.

00:05:16.660 --> 00:05:21.990
This includes the flame-topped Juche Tower,
which stands exactly one meter taller than

00:05:21.990 --> 00:05:27.949
the Washington Monument, all of its propaganda
posters, and the giant murals inside the Pyongyang

00:05:27.949 --> 00:05:30.050
Underground.

00:05:30.050 --> 00:05:36.280
In the 70s and 80s, Mansudae was also assigned
the job of constructing diplomatic gifts to

00:05:36.280 --> 00:05:38.780
current or potential allies.

00:05:38.780 --> 00:05:43.320
Ethiopia, for example, was given its Victory
Monument.

00:05:43.320 --> 00:05:49.050
Likewise, the presidential palace of Madagascar
was built by North Korea, completely free

00:05:49.050 --> 00:05:50.500
of charge.

00:05:50.500 --> 00:05:55.340
It was a cheap, easy way to win friends and
influence autocrats.

00:05:55.340 --> 00:06:01.919
Then, as with all things North Korean, a tectonic
shift occurred in the 90s.

00:06:01.919 --> 00:06:07.370
While the country was never completely the
obedient puppet state Moscow wanted it to

00:06:07.370 --> 00:06:12.740
be, it did very much rely on the Soviet Union
for material support.

00:06:12.740 --> 00:06:19.300
With its collapse, North Korea instantly lost
its biggest ally and financial backer.

00:06:19.300 --> 00:06:20.650
It needed cash.

00:06:20.650 --> 00:06:21.810
It needed a lot.

00:06:21.810 --> 00:06:24.960
And it needed it yesterday.

00:06:24.960 --> 00:06:29.410
The problem was, frankly, it didn’t have
all that much to offer.

00:06:29.410 --> 00:06:36.880
By 1990, South Korea’s GNP had grown to
$237 billion dollars to the North’s puny

00:06:36.880 --> 00:06:38.740
23.

00:06:38.740 --> 00:06:45.360
Faced with this existential predicament, it
turned to several unconventional gigs.

00:06:45.360 --> 00:06:51.830
In the intervening 3+ decades, the Mansudae
Art Studio had both honed its craft and grown

00:06:51.830 --> 00:06:57.539
to become one of, if not the largest art studios
in the world.

00:06:57.539 --> 00:07:03.910
Occupying about 30 acres in Pyongyang, its
campus contains 13 creative groups, seven

00:07:03.910 --> 00:07:06.860
factories, and 50 supply departments.

00:07:06.860 --> 00:07:14.130
There’s a paper mill, soccer stadium, kindergarten,
sauna, clinic, and gift shop.

00:07:14.130 --> 00:07:20.520
It has a reputation as the most prestigious
career path for promising North Korean students.

00:07:20.520 --> 00:07:27.180
Each of its 4,000 artists is expected to churn
out a steady stream of propaganda — 2 paintings

00:07:27.180 --> 00:07:29.150
a month, for example.

00:07:29.150 --> 00:07:35.850
According to a South Korean study, Kim family
artifacts, including all films, books, murals,

00:07:35.850 --> 00:07:41.070
statues, and so on, add up to 40% of the state’s
visible budget.

00:07:41.070 --> 00:07:47.110
So, while the Mansudae Overseas Projects division
was originally commissioned for the sole purpose

00:07:47.110 --> 00:07:54.470
of making allies, after the fall of the Soviet
Union, it took on a far more sinister purpose:

00:07:54.470 --> 00:07:59.310
generating foreign currency for the personal
bank accounts of the Kim family.

00:07:59.310 --> 00:08:05.390
The African Renaissance Monument, although
the tallest, is only one of dozens of projects.

00:08:05.390 --> 00:08:12.060
Altogether, they’ve earned an estimated
$160 million for the regime in the last ten

00:08:12.060 --> 00:08:13.350
years.

00:08:13.350 --> 00:08:17.410
One of its more loyal customers is the nation
of Namibia.

00:08:17.410 --> 00:08:24.130
It commissioned a 5,000-seat war memorial,
presidential palace, independence museum,

00:08:24.130 --> 00:08:31.160
military museum, Ministry of Defense headquarters,
munitions factory, and presidential statue.

00:08:31.160 --> 00:08:37.379
Property records indicate North Korea bought
a warehouse for around $120,000 US Dollars

00:08:37.379 --> 00:08:44.110
in 2004, and some locals say they still see
North Koreans come and go to this day.

00:08:44.110 --> 00:08:49.110
Its northern neighbor, Angola, is also a frequent
patron.

00:08:49.110 --> 00:08:54.239
In addition to a park and peace monument,
there’s the curiously-shaped mausoleum of

00:08:54.239 --> 00:08:58.199
its first president which locals gave the
nickname “Sputnik”.

00:08:58.199 --> 00:09:04.329
In the Congo, a statue of its third president
rose eyebrows for its suspicious likeness

00:09:04.329 --> 00:09:06.540
to the body of Kim Jong-Il.

00:09:06.540 --> 00:09:11.629
Some speculate it was a lazy “repurposing”,
though the artists can be forgiven given the

00:09:11.629 --> 00:09:14.769
lack of diversity involved in their day-to-day
work.

00:09:14.769 --> 00:09:20.879
In Zimbabwe, several of its statues generated
extra controversy thanks to its especially

00:09:20.879 --> 00:09:23.040
egregious past.

00:09:23.040 --> 00:09:28.399
In the 80s, North Korean-trained troops massacred
thousands of citizens after the Rhodesian

00:09:28.399 --> 00:09:30.220
Bush War.

00:09:30.220 --> 00:09:34.149
There are even a few examples outside Africa.

00:09:34.149 --> 00:09:41.149
Visitors to Cambodia’s Angkor Wat, for example,
may also stop by the nearby Panorama Museum,

00:09:41.149 --> 00:09:46.189
which was designed, built, and is now operated
by North Korea.

00:09:46.189 --> 00:09:54.220
Inside is a 120-meter long, 13-meter high,
360-degree mural featuring 45,000 people.

00:09:54.220 --> 00:10:01.620
It was painted by 63 artists over four months,
after spending over a year drawing the outline.

00:10:01.620 --> 00:10:08.129
In exchange, North Korea is entitled to 100%
of the proceeds for the first 10 years, followed

00:10:08.129 --> 00:10:12.100
by a 10 year 50-50 split with the Cambodian
government.

00:10:12.100 --> 00:10:19.489
Upon hearing all this, one might reasonably
ask: What’s in it for them?

00:10:19.489 --> 00:10:22.230
Mansudae is sanctioned by the United Nations.

00:10:22.230 --> 00:10:28.040
Doing business with it is also likely to anger
locals — why not purchase from a more reputable

00:10:28.040 --> 00:10:30.230
professional?

00:10:30.230 --> 00:10:36.649
One answer is best explained, strangely, by
a nondescript fountain in Frankfurt.

00:10:36.649 --> 00:10:44.500
This relatively small $264,000 Mansudae-built
sculpture is the only one built for a Western

00:10:44.500 --> 00:10:46.110
Democracy.

00:10:46.110 --> 00:10:51.290
When asked why North Korea was awarded the
contract, the commissioning museum director

00:10:51.290 --> 00:10:54.779
said it was simply a question of style.

00:10:54.779 --> 00:10:59.829
If you're looking to buy Socialist-Realist
art, no one in the world is better qualified

00:10:59.829 --> 00:11:01.009
to make it.

00:11:01.009 --> 00:11:07.559
North Korean artists have decades of experience
and all of it frozen in time.

00:11:07.559 --> 00:11:12.889
The second reason is just that it’s very,
very cheap.

00:11:12.889 --> 00:11:18.850
As captive citizens, its laborers are forced
to accept criminally low wages.

00:11:18.850 --> 00:11:23.699
In other words: There’s plenty of demand
around the world for propaganda and the Kim

00:11:23.699 --> 00:11:28.779
regime has both plenty of supply and experience.

00:11:28.779 --> 00:11:34.850
As the cost of sanctions violations increase,
some countries will find the risks too high.

00:11:34.850 --> 00:11:41.420
Others, however, will surely only do so more
covertly — dragging the most secretive country

00:11:41.420 --> 00:11:46.180
in the world even further into the shadows.

00:11:46.180 --> 00:11:51.749
Such is the dilemma of sanctions: Impose too
many and offenders will only retreat further

00:11:51.749 --> 00:11:53.990
from the international order.

00:11:53.990 --> 00:11:59.610
How do you, as a country, punish without permanently
excluding?

